// VoxelFluidCollision.ush -- particle vs terrain, over the occupancy bitfield.
//
// The two entry points VoxelFluidContract.ush:70-76 promises, and nothing
// else. This is the ONLY file that reads FluidOccupancyBits; the solver calls
// VoxelFluidResolveCollision and never touches a bit itself, which is what
// keeps the packing in one place (VoxelFluidOccupancy.usf writes it, this
// reads it, voxelcore/fluidoccupancy.h defines it and vxc_tests proves it).
//
// Part of Phase 0 spike (c), docs/water-rearchitecture-plan-2026-08-09.md.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE CPU REFERENCE IS voxelcore/fluidoccupancy.h AND THIS MIRRORS IT
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Every constant and every branch below has a named counterpart there, and
// tests/test_fluidoccupancy.cpp pins the behaviour -- including a
// cross-check against voxelcore/raycast.h, the project's authoritative integer
// Amanatides & Woo walk, on the same geometry. The mirror is maintained by
// hand, exactly as worldgen.ush mirrors amplifier.h. If you change a rule
// here, change it there, and the test tells you whether you got it right
// without launching an editor.
//
// Conventions taken verbatim from raycast.h so there is one set in the repo:
//   faceAxis  0=x 1=y 2=z, and -1 when the segment STARTS inside solid, so no
//             face was crossed (raycast.h:22-24)
//   faceSign  +1 == entered moving in +axis, i.e. through the voxel's -axis
//             face (raycast.h:20-22)
//   ties      simultaneous crossings step ONE axis at a time, lowest first
//             (raycast.h:74-80)
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE ONE THING THE HOST MUST GUARANTEE
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// FluidOriginUU (the contract's float precision origin, :20-25) MUST equal
// FluidVolumeOriginVoxel * VOXEL_FLUID_VOXEL_UU.
//
// That is: the origin particle positions are relative to IS the volume's own
// minimum corner. Then a position maps to a voxel by one floor and one integer
// add, with no extra binding to thread through and nothing for the solver and
// the collision pass to disagree about. The alternative -- letting the two
// origins drift apart -- needs a third quantity that both passes must be given
// consistently, and the failure mode of getting it wrong is water colliding
// with terrain that is offset by a few metres, which reads as a worldgen bug.
//
// It costs nothing. Both origins only move when the active region recentres,
// and the extra work is one constant subtraction over <=300k particles --
// FluidRebaseParticlesMain in VoxelFluidOccupancy.usf, contract item 8.
// FVoxelFluidOccupancyVolume::GetOriginUU() (VoxelFluidOccupancy.h) is the
// host-side accessor that keeps the two in step, and it is the value the solver
// must rebase to.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE VOLUME IS TOROIDAL (contract item 4)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// The 16 MiB buffer is a ROLLING WINDOW, not a flat array indexed by window
// position. Three coordinate spaces, and the reason to hold them apart:
//
//   WORLD    planet voxel coordinates -- what the solid predicate is asked
//            about.
//   LOCAL    world minus FluidVolumeOriginVoxel, 0..511. THE GEOMETRIC SPACE:
//            particle positions are local UU, the walk's boundary planes are at
//            local voxel edges, and the collision projection returns a local
//            position. Nothing in this file's geometry changed.
//   STORAGE  local plus FluidVolumeWrapOffsetVoxel, modulo 512 -- where the bit
//            physically lives. Used by exactly one expression in this file, in
//            VoxelFluidSolidAtVoxel, and nowhere else.
//
// WHY. Flat addressing renames every bit in the buffer when the origin moves,
// so a recentre had to discard all 16 MiB and refill 262,144 bricks -- a
// multi-second stall with the water frozen inside it, which is why v0 latched
// the origin once and never moved. With storage anchored to the window's
// accumulated motion instead, the bits still in view keep their addresses; only
// the entering slab is invalidated (1/8 of the volume for one 64-voxel step),
// and the exiting slab is not cleared or copied -- the entering slab lands on
// exactly its addresses. voxelcore/fluidoccupancy.h carries the full argument,
// including why the offset is accumulated motion rather than the simpler
// `world % 512` (the latter needs a 32-aligned origin, and the origin is the
// camera's voxel minus 256).

#ifndef VOXEL_FLUID_COLLISION_USH
#define VOXEL_FLUID_COLLISION_USH

#include "/VoxelEarth/VoxelFluidContract.ush"

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Constants (mirror of voxelcore/fluidoccupancy.h)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// One 10 cm terrain voxel in UU. This is vxc::kVoxelSizeMm (core.h:263) in UU
// and is NOT the contract's VOXEL_FLUID_REST_SPACING_UU: the two are equal
// today and mean different things (terrain lattice vs PBF rest spacing), so
// neither is defined in terms of the other. Mirror of vxc::kFluidVoxelUU.
#define VOXEL_FLUID_VOXEL_UU  10.0f

// THE ANTI-TUNNELLING BUDGET. Mirror of vxc::kFluidMaxCollisionSteps, and the
// number the spike exists to justify.
//
//   Motion per substep is v*dt UU. The walk crosses one voxel boundary per
//   step and a diagonal segment crosses up to sqrt(3) times as many boundaries
//   as an axis-aligned one of the same length, so the budget covers
//
//       v_max = VOXEL_FLUID_MAX_COLLISION_STEPS * VOXEL_FLUID_VOXEL_UU
//               / (dt * sqrt(3))          (worst case, diagonal)
//
//   Against the plan's worst case -- a 40 m waterfall, v = sqrt(2*g*h) =
//   sqrt(2*9.81*40) = 28.0 m/s = 2800 UU/s -- and PBF substeps of 1/240..1/60:
//
//       steps=8,  dt=1/60   ->  2771 UU/s   FAILS the 40 m waterfall by 1 %
//       steps=16, dt=1/60   ->  5543 UU/s = 55.4 m/s, a 157 m fall
//       steps=16, dt=1/120  -> 11086 UU/s = 111 m/s,  a 627 m fall
//       steps=16, dt=1/240  -> 22170 UU/s = 222 m/s
//
//   8 is the obvious budget and it misses the plan's own named case at the
//   slow end of the substep range. 16 clears it by 1.98x there -- a 157 m fall
//   -- and costs nothing typical: the walk exits at the first solid voxel or
//   when the segment is spent, so a particle drifting at 1 m/s does ONE
//   iteration. test_fluidoccupancy.cpp asserts the waterfall case, the exact
//   cap, and the "8 would have failed" arithmetic, so these cannot rot.
//
//   RAISING IT IS ALMOST FREE. The loop is [loop], not unrolled, so a bigger
//   budget adds no code and no cost to particles that exit early; 24 steps
//   covers 83 m/s (a 352 m fall) and 32 covers 111 m/s (a 627 m fall). 16 is
//   chosen because 40 m is the stated requirement and going over budget clamps
//   rather than leaks -- not because 16 is a limit.
#define VOXEL_FLUID_MAX_COLLISION_STEPS  16

// AND THE BUDGET IS NOT A LEAK. Running out of steps with segment remaining
// clamps the particle to the last boundary the walk VERIFIED, rather than
// letting it continue. Above the supported speed a particle is SLOWED, never
// passed through a wall -- so the numbers above are a speed limit and not a
// tunnelling threshold, and the visible symptom of getting them wrong is
// sluggish water instead of invisibly leaking water.

// One resolve pass clamps one axis; a corner needs up to three, and cannot
// need a fourth. Mirror of vxc::kFluidMaxResolveIterations.
#define VOXEL_FLUID_MAX_RESOLVE_ITERATIONS  3

// How far outside the face the projected position sits. 0.01 UU = 0.1 mm.
// Sized against float precision, not physics: the volume is 5120 UU across, so
// origin-relative positions reach ~5120 where a float32 ULP is ~4.9e-4 UU. The
// skin is ~20 ULP -- big enough that re-flooring the corrected position lands
// in the free voxel rather than back on the boundary, small enough to be
// invisible at 1/1000 of a voxel. Mirror of vxc::kFluidCollisionSkinUU.
#define VOXEL_FLUID_COLLISION_SKIN_UU  0.01f

// "This axis never crosses a boundary." Any value above 1 works (the walk
// clamps at t>1); finite rather than inf so it survives arithmetic.
#define VOXEL_FLUID_NO_CROSSING_T  1e30f

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Bindings (VoxelFluidContract.ush:64-69)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Declared here rather than in the contract because the contract is included
// by the WRITER too (VoxelFluidOccupancy.usf), which needs the RWBuffer form
// of the same name. One name, one meaning, two access modes, and the two
// declarations can never meet: nothing includes both this file and the
// occupancy kernel.
Buffer<uint> FluidOccupancyBits;
// World voxel coordinate of volume-LOCAL (0,0,0) -- the window's min corner.
int3 FluidVolumeOriginVoxel;
// 512. A uniform rather than a literal so a future half-resolution or
// quarter-extent volume is a host change, not a shader edit; every use below
// derives from it. MUST BE A POWER OF TWO: the toroidal wrap below masks
// instead of dividing (mirror of the static_assert on
// vxc::kFluidVolumeWrapMask).
uint FluidVolumeDimVoxels;
// TOROIDAL ADDRESSING (contract item 4). The STORAGE coordinate of local
// (0,0,0): how far the rolling window has slid, modulo the volume. Zero for a
// window that has never moved, always in [0, Dim) and always a multiple of the
// recentre step (vxc::kFluidRecentreStepVoxels, 64) so no 32-voxel output word
// straddles the seam.
uint3 FluidVolumeWrapOffsetVoxel;

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The bit read
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// LOCAL (window) voxel -> STORAGE voxel, the whole of what toroidal addressing
// is. `local` is already known to be in [0, Dim) at every call site, and the
// offset likewise, so the sum is below 2*Dim and the mask is one conditional
// subtract -- no negative operand ever reaches it and no integer modulo is
// needed. Mirror of vxc::fluidVolumeStorageAxis.
uint3 VoxelFluidStorageVoxel(uint3 localVoxel)
{
	// lint-shader-ub: allow UNSIGNED_UNDERFLOW - FluidVolumeDimVoxels is the volume edge, uploaded by the host as vxc::kFluidVolumeDimVoxels (512) and static_asserted to be a positive power of two in both VoxelFluidOccupancy.cpp and fluidoccupancy.h; it is never 0, so Dim-1 is the wrap mask 511 and cannot underflow
	return (localVoxel + FluidVolumeWrapOffsetVoxel) & (FluidVolumeDimVoxels - 1u);
}

// OUTSIDE THE VOLUME IS SOLID. This is the rule the whole collision model
// rests on (VoxelFluidContract.ush:70-73): a particle can never escape through
// an unbuilt edge, the same "blocked, never guessed" convention the fine
// terrain tier uses. The opposite convention drains a river into the void the
// first time the volume lags the camera by a frame, and it looks like a solver
// bug rather than a residency bug.
//
// AND THE WINDOW TEST IS NOT THE WRAP. The bounds check below runs on the
// LOCAL coordinate and is exactly what it was before toroidal addressing; the
// wrap happens after it, only to find where the bit is stored. Deleting the
// check and trusting the modulo to "wrap harmlessly" is the one way to get this
// wrong that still compiles: a particle one voxel past the west face would
// collide against the terrain 51.2 m to its east, which reads as worldgen and
// not as a bug.
//
// Mirror of vxc::fluidSolidAtVoxel.
bool VoxelFluidSolidAtVoxel(int3 worldVoxel)
{
	const int3 l = worldVoxel - FluidVolumeOriginVoxel;
	const int dim = (int)FluidVolumeDimVoxels;
	if (any(l < 0) || any(l >= dim))
	{
		return true;
	}

	// x-major packing: 32 voxels per word along x, rows along x then y then z --
	// over STORAGE coordinates, which is the only place local and storage differ.
	const uint3 s = VoxelFluidStorageVoxel((uint3)l);
	const uint wordsPerRow = FluidVolumeDimVoxels >> 5u;
	const uint index = (s.z * FluidVolumeDimVoxels + s.y) * wordsPerRow + (s.x >> 5u);
	// lint-shader-ub: allow VARIABLE_SHIFT - the distance is masked to 0..31 by the & 31u in the expression itself, so it is provably below the 32-bit left operand's width
	return (FluidOccupancyBits[index] & (1u << (s.x & 31u))) != 0u;
}

// Volume-local UU -> world voxel. One floor and one integer add, which is what
// the FluidOriginUU == FluidVolumeOriginVoxel * VOXEL_FLUID_VOXEL_UU rule at
// the top of this file buys.
int3 VoxelFluidVoxelForPosUU(float3 posUU)
{
	return (int3)floor(posUU / VOXEL_FLUID_VOXEL_UU) + FluidVolumeOriginVoxel;
}

bool VoxelFluidSolidAtPosUU(float3 posUU)
{
	return VoxelFluidSolidAtVoxel(VoxelFluidVoxelForPosUU(posUU));
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The voxel-line walk
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

struct FVoxelFluidWalkHit
{
	bool  Hit;
	int3  Voxel;          // first solid voxel entered, WORLD coordinates
	int   FaceAxis;       // 0/1/2, or -1 when the segment started inside solid
	int   FaceSign;       // +1 == entered moving in +axis
	float TEnter;         // segment parameter of the crossing, or of the last
	                      // verified boundary when BudgetExhausted
	bool  StartedInside;
	bool  BudgetExhausted;
};

// Float Amanatides & Woo from prevPosUU to posUU. Mirror of
// vxc::fluidWalkVoxelLine.
//
// WHY A WALK AND NOT AN ENDPOINT TEST. A PBF substep can move a particle
// several voxels; testing only the destination sails straight through a 10 cm
// wall whenever the far side happens to be air, which is every wall. The walk
// is what makes "a 10 cm wall stops a particle at any plausible speed" true
// rather than true-at-low-speed.
FVoxelFluidWalkHit VoxelFluidWalkVoxelLine(float3 prevPosUU, float3 posUU)
{
	FVoxelFluidWalkHit outHit;
	outHit.Hit = false;
	outHit.FaceAxis = -1;
	outHit.FaceSign = 0;
	outHit.TEnter = 0.0f;
	outHit.StartedInside = false;
	outHit.BudgetExhausted = false;

	const int3 localStart = (int3)floor(prevPosUU / VOXEL_FLUID_VOXEL_UU);
	const int3 startVoxel = localStart + FluidVolumeOriginVoxel;
	// A plain array, not an int3: the walk indexes it by the axis it chose each
	// step, and dynamic component indexing of a VECTOR forces DXC to spill the
	// whole vector to scratch memory on every write. An array is already
	// addressable.
	int v[3] = { startVoxel.x, startVoxel.y, startVoxel.z };
	outHit.Voxel = startVoxel;

	if (VoxelFluidSolidAtVoxel(startVoxel))
	{
		// raycast.h:42-48's case: no face was crossed, so no projection
		// direction can be derived. Reported, never guessed.
		outHit.Hit = true;
		outHit.StartedInside = true;
		return outHit;
	}

	const float3 d = posUU - prevPosUU;

	float tMax[3], tDelta[3];
	int step[3];
	{
		const float pv[3] = { prevPosUU.x, prevPosUU.y, prevPosUU.z };
		const float dv[3] = { d.x, d.y, d.z };
		const float lv[3] = { (float)localStart.x, (float)localStart.y, (float)localStart.z };
		[unroll]
		for (int a = 0; a < 3; ++a)
		{
			if (dv[a] > 0.0f)
			{
				step[a] = 1;
				tMax[a] = ((lv[a] + 1.0f) * VOXEL_FLUID_VOXEL_UU - pv[a]) / dv[a];
				tDelta[a] = VOXEL_FLUID_VOXEL_UU / dv[a];
			}
			else if (dv[a] < 0.0f)
			{
				step[a] = -1;
				tMax[a] = (lv[a] * VOXEL_FLUID_VOXEL_UU - pv[a]) / dv[a];
				tDelta[a] = -VOXEL_FLUID_VOXEL_UU / dv[a];
			}
			else
			{
				step[a] = 0;
				tMax[a] = VOXEL_FLUID_NO_CROSSING_T;
				tDelta[a] = VOXEL_FLUID_NO_CROSSING_T;
			}
		}
	}

	float tLast = 0.0f;
	// [loop], not unrolled: 16 copies of a buffer load and a branch is a lot of
	// instruction cache for a loop that typically runs once.
	[loop]
	for (int i = 0; i < VOXEL_FLUID_MAX_COLLISION_STEPS; ++i)
	{
		// Ties step one axis at a time, lowest first -- raycast.h:74-80.
		int axis;
		if (tMax[0] <= tMax[1] && tMax[0] <= tMax[2])      { axis = 0; }
		else if (tMax[1] <= tMax[2])                       { axis = 1; }
		else                                               { axis = 2; }

		if (tMax[axis] > 1.0f)
		{
			return outHit;   // segment exhausted, nothing in the way
		}

		tLast = tMax[axis];
		v[axis] += step[axis];
		tMax[axis] += tDelta[axis];

		const int3 vNow = int3(v[0], v[1], v[2]);
		if (VoxelFluidSolidAtVoxel(vNow))
		{
			outHit.Hit = true;
			outHit.Voxel = vNow;
			outHit.FaceAxis = axis;
			outHit.FaceSign = step[axis];
			outHit.TEnter = tLast;
			return outHit;
		}
	}

	// Budget spent with segment left. The last voxel entered was tested and is
	// empty, so tLast is a verified-safe place to stop.
	outHit.BudgetExhausted = true;
	outHit.TEnter = tLast;
	return outHit;
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Resolution
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

struct FVoxelFluidCollision
{
	float3 PosUU;        // corrected position
	float3 Normal;       // outward normal(s) of the blocking face(s), or 0
	bool   Blocked;
	bool   StartedInside;
	bool   SpeedClamped;  // the step budget capped the motion
	int    Iterations;    // resolve passes that clamped an axis; 0 == free
};

// The full result. Mirror of vxc::fluidResolveCollision.
//
// WHY IT ITERATES. One pass clamps one axis, and the clamped position can
// still lie inside a different solid voxel -- a particle driven into a corner
// has to come out of two or three faces. Each pass clamps a NEW axis (the mask
// refuses a repeat), so the loop is bounded by three and terminates by
// construction rather than by a tolerance.
FVoxelFluidCollision VoxelFluidResolveCollisionFull(float3 posUU, float3 prevPosUU)
{
	FVoxelFluidCollision res;
	res.PosUU = posUU;
	res.Normal = float3(0.0f, 0.0f, 0.0f);
	res.Blocked = false;
	res.StartedInside = false;
	res.SpeedClamped = false;
	res.Iterations = 0;

	uint clampedAxes = 0u;

	[loop]
	for (int iter = 0; iter < VOXEL_FLUID_MAX_RESOLVE_ITERATIONS; ++iter)
	{
		const FVoxelFluidWalkHit h = VoxelFluidWalkVoxelLine(prevPosUU, res.PosUU);
		res.Iterations = iter + 1;

		if (h.StartedInside)
		{
			// Nowhere sound to project to. Hold: the particle stops where it
			// was instead of being teleported somewhere plausible. This is
			// also what a particle inside a not-yet-built region does, since
			// unbuilt reads as solid.
			res.PosUU = prevPosUU;
			res.Blocked = true;
			res.StartedInside = true;
			return res;
		}

		if (h.BudgetExhausted)
		{
			res.PosUU = prevPosUU + (res.PosUU - prevPosUU) * h.TEnter;
			res.SpeedClamped = true;
			return res;
		}

		if (!h.Hit)
		{
			res.Iterations = iter;   // this pass found nothing; motion is free
			return res;
		}

		// lint-shader-ub: allow VARIABLE_SHIFT - FaceAxis is 0, 1 or 2 here: the only other value it can hold is -1 for StartedInside, and that branch returned above, as did !Hit
		const uint axisBit = 1u << (uint)h.FaceAxis;
		if ((clampedAxes & axisBit) != 0u)
		{
			// Same axis twice. Unreachable with the projection below (the
			// clamp plus skin puts the position in the free voxel along that
			// axis), so getting here means the volume changed under us
			// mid-resolve or the skin is too small. Hold rather than loop.
			res.PosUU = prevPosUU;
			res.Blocked = true;
			return res;
		}
		clampedAxes |= axisBit;

		// The blocking face plane, in volume-local UU. FaceSign +1 means the
		// walk came in through the voxel's LOW face, so the free side is below.
		const int3 localVoxel = h.Voxel - FluidVolumeOriginVoxel;
		const int localV = (h.FaceAxis == 0) ? localVoxel.x
		                 : (h.FaceAxis == 1) ? localVoxel.y
		                                     : localVoxel.z;

		float corrected;
		float normalComponent;
		if (h.FaceSign > 0)
		{
			corrected = (float)localV * VOXEL_FLUID_VOXEL_UU - VOXEL_FLUID_COLLISION_SKIN_UU;
			normalComponent = -1.0f;
		}
		else
		{
			corrected = ((float)localV + 1.0f) * VOXEL_FLUID_VOXEL_UU + VOXEL_FLUID_COLLISION_SKIN_UU;
			normalComponent = 1.0f;
		}

		if (h.FaceAxis == 0)      { res.PosUU.x = corrected; res.Normal.x = normalComponent; }
		else if (h.FaceAxis == 1) { res.PosUU.y = corrected; res.Normal.y = normalComponent; }
		else                      { res.PosUU.z = corrected; res.Normal.z = normalComponent; }

		res.Blocked = true;
	}

	return res;
}

// THE CONTRACT ENTRY POINT (VoxelFluidContract.ush:74-76). Projects a
// penetrating particle out along the face normal of the voxel it entered and
// returns the corrected position.
//
// ON "KILLING THE NORMAL VELOCITY COMPONENT". PBF derives velocity as
// (x - x_prev)/dt, so projecting x is most of the job -- but it leaves a
// residual approach velocity of (penetration depth)/dt along the normal,
// because x_prev is still on the far side. To kill it exactly the solver must
// also lift x_prev's normal component onto the projected plane, and that needs
// the normal, which this signature has nowhere to return.
//
// So: use VoxelFluidResolveCollisionEx below in the solver's integrate step.
// This wrapper exists because the contract names it, it is correct as a pure
// position projection, and a caller that only needs "put it back outside"
// should not have to declare two out-parameters.
float3 VoxelFluidResolveCollision(float3 posUU, float3 prevPosUU)
{
	return VoxelFluidResolveCollisionFull(posUU, prevPosUU).PosUU;
}

// The form the PBF integrate step should call.
//
//   float3 n;
//   bool blocked;
//   pos = VoxelFluidResolveCollisionEx(pos, prevPos, n, blocked);
//   if (blocked)
//   {
//       // exact zero normal velocity: move prevPos onto the same plane, then
//       // the solver's own v = (pos - prevPos)/dt has no normal component.
//       prevPos -= n * dot(prevPos - pos, n);
//   }
//
// Tangential motion is untouched, which is the free-slip wall the blueprint's
// collision model asks for. A friction term, if one is ever wanted, belongs in
// the solver on the tangential remainder -- not here.
float3 VoxelFluidResolveCollisionEx(float3 posUU, float3 prevPosUU,
                                    out float3 outNormal, out bool outBlocked)
{
	const FVoxelFluidCollision res = VoxelFluidResolveCollisionFull(posUU, prevPosUU);
	outNormal = res.Normal;
	outBlocked = res.Blocked;
	return res.PosUU;
}

#endif // VOXEL_FLUID_COLLISION_USH
